# Never push to main

Agents must **never** push to `main` (or `master`) on any remote — including
`gitlab` and `origin`.

## Required workflow

1. Create or switch to a **feature branch** (never commit intended work only on
   `main` if it will be pushed).
2. Commit on that branch with `git commit -s` — every commit needs a DCO
   sign-off trailer (see `commit-sign-off.mdc`).
3. Push the **feature branch** only: `git push -u gitlab HEAD` or
   `git push -u gitlab <branch-name>`.
4. Open a **GitLab merge request** into `main`. Do not merge unless the user
   explicitly asks.

## Explicit bans

- `git push … main`, `git push … HEAD:main`, or any refspec that updates
  `refs/heads/main` / `refs/heads/master` on a remote.
- Force-pushing `main` / `master` unless the user **explicitly** requests that
  exact force update in the current message (and even then, prefer
  `--force-with-lease` and confirm the target SHA).
- Treating “commit and push” as permission to push `main`. That phrase means
  push the **current feature branch**.

## If already on main locally

When the user asks to commit/push and `git branch --show-current` is `main`:

1. Create a feature branch from the commits (or before committing).
2. Push that branch.
3. Point the user at an MR URL.

Do **not** push `main` “just this once.”

